{"tag":"34FJ","body":"<ol class=\"pq-parts\" role=\"list\"><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (a)\">a</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Suppose <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"U\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"V\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span></span></span></span></span> are two utility functions for <span class=\"m-nb\"><span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"≿\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.9592em;vertical-align:-0.2296em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel amsrm\">≿</span></span></span></span></span>.</span> Must there exist a strictly monotonic <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"f : ℝ → ℝ\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span></span></span></span></span></span> such that <span class=\"m-nb\"><span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"V(x) = f(U(x))\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">))</span></span></span></span></span></span>?</span></p></div></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (b)\">b</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>May a utility function for a continuous preference relation fail to be continuous?</p></div></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (c)\">c</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Take <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"X = ℝ\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span></span></span></span></span></span> and the utility <span class=\"m-nb\"><span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"u(x) = ⌊ x ⌋\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">⌊</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">⌋</span></span></span></span></span></span>.</span> Prove that the preference it induces fails continuity.</p></div></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (d)\">d</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Prove that continuity in the sense of C1 or C2 holds exactly when C3 holds (for each <span class=\"m-nb\"><span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"x\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span></span>,</span> both <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"y : y ≿ x\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel amsrm\">≿</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"y : x ≿ y\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel amsrm\">≿</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span></span></span></span></span></span> are closed), and exactly when C4 holds (for each <span class=\"m-nb\"><span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"x\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span></span>,</span> both <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"y : y ≻ x\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≻</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"y : x ≻ y\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≻</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span></span></span></span></span></span> are open).</p></div></div></li></ol><div class=\"pq-ans pq-ans--whole\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>(a) <strong>No</strong>: the claim is refuted, even allowing <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"f\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span></span></span></span></span> to be strictly increasing or strictly decreasing. (b) <strong>Yes</strong>: a continuous preference can have a discontinuous representation. (c) Proved: the floor-function preference is not continuous. (d) Proved: C1 is equivalent to C3 and to C4, and with C1 ⟺ C2 from the lecture all four definitions coincide.</p></div></div>"}